---
description: Reply and think in Arabic, and shape it for the Cursor CLI cell screen.
alwaysApply: true
---

Always answer the user in Arabic (Modern Standard Arabic, or the dialect the user writes in).
Keep code, terminal commands, file paths, identifiers and English-only technical terms in English, inside backticks.
Prefer bullet lists over markdown tables.
Do your internal reasoning / thinking in Arabic as well.
The Cursor CLI draws one character per cell from left to right. Logical Arabic then looks disconnected and reversed.
Follow the `Arabic display:` line when one is present.
When it says `native`, write Arabic in normal logical order and do not run a shape command.
When it says `visual`, or when no Arabic display line is present, you must run the shaper before the user can see the reply. Do not answer with Arabic letters first. Write the reply with the file tool to `$env:TEMP\cursor-arabic-reply.txt`. If the session has a `Shape command:` line, run that exact command. Otherwise run this exact PowerShell command:

[Console]::InputEncoding = [Console]::OutputEncoding = $OutputEncoding = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false); node "$env:USERPROFILE\.cursor\plugins\local\arabic\hooks\shape.mjs" "$env:TEMP\cursor-arabic-reply.txt" "$env:TEMP\cursor-arabic-shaped.txt"

Do not put Arabic in that command or in a PowerShell pipeline. Windows PowerShell 5.1 turns Arabic in pipes or mishandled stdout into `????`. Never paste logical Arabic into the chat as the final answer. After the command, read `$env:TEMP\cursor-arabic-shaped.txt` with the file tool. Ignore shell stdout (ASCII `ok` only). The user-visible message is only that shaped file, copied exactly, including leading spaces. It must not contain Arabic letters from ء to ي. Rows of `?` mean the file path was skipped. Disconnected letters such as م ر ح ب ا mean the shaper did not run. If the command fails or permission is declined, say so instead of pasting the logical reply.
